WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.161 --> 00:00:03.390
(light music)

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.160
- [Narrator] Japan's train system ranked

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.400
by Statista is the most
efficient in the world.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.410
The U.S. though is far behind.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.720
It's tied for 11th best.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.120
Roughly 15 million people ride the subway

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.280
each day in Japan's capital city,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.120
around 11 million more daily
riders than New York subway.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.520
We compared the Tokyo and New York subways

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.440
to find out how Japan's rail system

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.903
got so far ahead of the United States.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.330
Take a look at this map of
New York City subway system.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.170
Now, compare that to Tokyo's
map, a key distinction

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.440
where the public transit
connections intersect.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.500
In New York, public transit
commuters into Manhattan

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.680
likely only have these
options to disembark.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.440
Japan's map has more points of connection.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.410
- What's really distinctive about Tokyo

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.650
and actually Japanese
cities more generally

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.930
is they all interconnect.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.490
- [Narrator] Commuter
rails connect directly

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:00.810
with city subway tracks,

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.840
which reduces congestion
and unnecessary transfers.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.580
- If you just ride the train all the way

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.020
out to your final destination,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.510
you don't have to be paying attention

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.400
to your transfer station.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.780
You just fall asleep and you know

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.200
when you're gonna wake
up near your station.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.820
- [Narrator] These easy commutes are key

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.710
for the 2.4 million people

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.830
traveling into Tokyo daily
from outside the city.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:26.070
On top of that, Tokyo Wides
only need one of two cards

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.680
to pay for most public
transportation systems,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.370
not just in the city, but
also in many parts of Japan.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:33.480
And in New York-

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.520
- You have to often have a different card

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.900
to get on any of the ridership system.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.110
It's not like an integrated
pay system or governing system.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.120
- [Narrator] So you have to
use all these different tickets

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.130
to ride the public rail systems.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.220
In some cases, these separate
rail systems are the result

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.250
of different jurisdictions
that oversee transit.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.770
The subway, for example,
is owned by the city

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.680
and leased to the Metropolitan
Transportation Authority,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.920
better known as the MTA,
which in turn is controlled

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.720
by the New York State government.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.420
And New Jersey Transit, which
connects to New York City,

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.900
is run by the New Jersey
Department of Transportation.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.000
While New York doesn't
have fully integrated

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.750
public transit connections,
some aspects are improving.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.190
First is payment.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.350
- We now have the ability to pay

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.030
with a credit card when
you go into a subway,

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.070
so you don't have to have
a special metro card,

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.440
which will be made obsolete eventually.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.500
I think the goal is to
eventually have one fee

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.800
in one rail system, and that
will pay you through all of it.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.540
- [Narrator] Then there's
the profitability.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.970
Other than in 2020 and 2021,

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.640
during the COVID-19 pandemic, Tokyo Metro,

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.240
the largest subway company in
the city, has been profitable.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.890
Compare that to the MTA,

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.170
which historically operates at a loss.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:48.170
Its outstanding debt is forecast
to hit $47 billion by 2026.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.220
A key reason for this difference,

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.170
central government funding.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.930
Japan heavily invests in
public transportation.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.740
The national government owns
about 53% of Tokyo Metro,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.640
and the Metropolitan Government
of Tokyo owns about 47%.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:03.750
- After the second world war,

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.820
there was a continuous investment

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.280
in the public transit system,

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.690
which is primarily the
subways, the JR system,

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.810
and the private commuter rail
systems that all interconnect.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:20.760
There's never been a let
up in continued investment

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.360
in adding lines and
making them work better.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.830
- [Narrator] The MTA, on the other hand,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.140
is not owned by other private companies.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.440
Its funding comes from multiple
places, including taxes,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.290
the city and state governments,
and some federal funding.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.260
The MTA also makes money by selling bonds.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:38.490
- It's one of the top issuers

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:41.370
of municipal bonds in the entire U.S.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:42.960
- [Narrator] One of the
largest buckets of funding

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.150
for both the New York and Tokyo Subways

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.100
comes directly from riders.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.210
In Japan's capital,

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.580
companies have flexibility to charge more.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.710
- It's all fair by distance,

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:54.720
so you get your fair at the end.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.760
- [Narrator] So depending
on which system you take,

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.260
the cost for riders can be
anywhere from 170 to 430 yen,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.090
which converts to about $1.27 to $3.22.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.370
The main New York subway fares are flat,

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:11.940
though the LIRR and Metro
North prices vary by zone.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.210
- Right now, about 40%
of the MTA's funding

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:16.470
comes from the fare box,

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.260
from what the riders pay to ride.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:20.610
That number has gone down a lot,

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.160
which is why we're
redoing the funding model.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.750
- [Narrator] Because
many white collar workers

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.670
have worked from home since 2020,

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.850
there have been less riders
and thus less revenue.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.970
This has worsened the
MTA's financial situation.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.670
To help fill the funding gap
and account for inflation,

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.120
prices will increase slightly from $2.75

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.660
to around $2.86 later this year.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.720
In Japan, public
transportation has a reputation

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.490
for being staunchly on time.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.790
In 2017, one commuter
rail connecting Tokyo

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.710
to Tsukuba famously apologized

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.200
for having a train leave 20 seconds early,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.050
but the Tokyo Subways
can face some hiccups.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:01.530
Tokyo Metro post delays
from the past 35 days.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.960
In April of this year, several train lines

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.880
were running about 10 minutes late.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.400
In New York City, delays are common.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.760
In 2022, New York City
passengers waited an average

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.190
of an additional 64 minutes on platforms

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:15.720
cumulatively each month,

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.750
as well as an extra 18
minutes on the train.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:21.480
But the MTA says that's an improvement.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.030
- We have right now the
best on time performance

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.610
of our subway system in over 10 years.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.250
It may not be up to Asia's
standards, but for New York,

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.650
it's really a great step
in the right direction.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.240
We have an old system,

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.550
so there are things that
go wrong mechanically.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:39.630
You have a huge system of
switches and interlocking,

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.270
which require a ton of maintenance.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.800
- [Narrator] Japan's history of investment

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.960
in public transportation gives Tokyo

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.000
the upper hand for its rail system.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.210
- It would take years, if not decades,

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:55.650
of very significant investment
in the New York subway system

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:59.730
for it to match the levels of efficiency

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:03.090
as the Tokyo subway system
because they've been working hard

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:05.960
on that for the last 60 years.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:08.460
(light music)

